# Build outputs
bin/
obj/
Debug/
Release/

# Visual Studio
.vs/
*.user
*.suo
*.userosscache
*.sln.docstates

# NuGet
*.nupkg
packages/

# Python
__pycache__/
*.pyc
*.pyo
*.pyd
.env
venv/
env/
.pytest_cache/

# Logs and temp
*.log
*.tmp
debug_chunk.wav
# Unanchored and globbed: the live cache AND any archived snapshot of it. An exact
# "ais_cache.json" missed ais_cache-wide-2026-08-13.json, which holds 13,571 real vessel
# records -- the same class of leak that put three reference files on the remote.
ais_cache*.json
identified_vessels.html
# Globbed for the same reason as ais_cache*.json above: the exact name missed dated
# snapshots. The store holds full transcripts of real traffic, so a snapshot leaks as
# much as the live file.
conversations*.json
# ...and the timestamped backups a backfill leaves beside it, which the pattern above misses
# because the name does not end in .json. Same transcripts, same leak.
conversations.json.bak-*
# bench_identify --suggest output. Unlike the other bench-*.json, which carry only
# timestamps, MMSIs and outcomes, each shortlist row quotes the FRAGMENT that matched
# ("heard": "MELD THEM IN") -- words actually spoken on air, against a time and a ship.
suggest-*.json
# ais_station_count.py output: per-hour MMSI observations from the local receiver, i.e. a
# record of which vessels were where and when. Unanchored, like the patterns below, because
# the anchored form is exactly what let three reference files reach the remote.
ais-station-count*.jsonl

# Reference transcripts built from your own recordings.
#
# No transcript file is tracked, not even a synthetic one: transcripts are the content of
# radio communications not addressed to you, and recording or republishing them is
# restricted in many countries (NL: Telecommunicatiewet art. 18.13, ITU RR 17.3). The
# format is documented in docs/user-manual.md; build your own with make_references.py.
references.txt
references-*.txt
# Timestamped backups written before any bulk correction pass -- same transcript data,
# same restriction.
references*.bak-*
identification-labels*.bak-*
identification-labels-verified.txt

# bench_identify.py ground truth, and its output. Drafted from conversations.json, so the
# note field carries transcript text and it falls under exactly the restriction above.
identification-labels*.txt
bench-identify-results*.json
# --repeats writes one of these per run set. Same reason as the line above: the per-turn
# rows carry real vessel names and MMSIs, and this repo is public.
bench-identify-repeats*.json
# Draft and scored transcript runs over replayed IQ arms -- these are verbatim recordings of
# real vessel traffic, which is the same reason the golden DSP fixtures are not committed.
# score-*.json additionally embeds the hand-verified reference for every clip.
draft-*.json
draft-*.html
score-*.json
score-*.html

# bench.py / stress.py output (generated locally; captures are user data)
bench-results*.json
bench-report*.html
stress-*.json
captures/

# bench_correct.py output. Both carry transcript text and fall under the same restriction
# as references*.txt above: the results files embed every corrected transcript per clip,
# and the cache holds each model's raw reply verbatim.
bench-correct-results*.json
bench-correct-cache/

# Secrets — never commit API keys
start-all.bat

# Claude Code local settings
.claude/

# Reference project (not part of this codebase)
WhisperPlugin/

# Few-shot examples are built from real exchanges, which are received radio traffic.
# Unanchored (no server/ prefix): a pattern containing a slash is anchored to the directory
# holding this .gitignore, so "server/*fewshot*.json" only ever matched files directly in
# server/ and silently let server/stt_proxy/conversation-fewshot.json through. "*" also never
# crosses a "/", so these must stay prefix-free to match at any depth.
*fewshot*.json
*examples*.json

# bench_conversation_correct.py output. Same restriction as bench-correct-results*.json above,
# and the same anchoring flaw as above -- unanchored so it matches at any depth.
bench-conv-correct-*
